## Docker/ VaultWarden / Caddy

I was doing research and wanted to learn Docker itself, the tools that run containers, and add Vaultwarden, a password vault application running in a container.

Vaultwarden is an open-source and self-hosted version of the Bitwarden server. It's a password manager where you can store passwords, 2FA codes, passkeys, and so much more, like credit cards and SSH keys. A couple of days ago, I actually got an e-mail from Google saying that some of my passwords might have been taken or breached, so this will allow me to keep my passwords from every single website that I've been creating accounts on secret, private, and within my own network so it will not be breached by hackers or threat actors. 

Caddy is a web server, but the specific thing that I want to build around it will be a feature called HTTPS. Normally, when setting up HTTPS, I manually have to get a certificate, install it and remember to renew it before it expires. Caddy handles all of that on its own; it requests a certificate, keeps it renewed, and redirects regular HTTP traffic to HTTPS automatically without me having to manage it hand by hand. Caddy's going to be sitting between my browser and my phone and Vaultwarden. When a request comes in for my domain, which will be on DuckDNS, Caddy is going to be the one that will actually terminate the HTTPS connection, meaning it's the piece proving yes this is really your server using its certificate, and then it will forward the request behind the scenes to Vaultwarden itself never has to deal with the certificates at all. Detox plane to HTTP to Caddy internally, and Caddy is the only thing exposed on port 443.



