2. I want to confirm that the group was created, and I will add myself to it.

I will type this in the terminal: "sudo usermod -aG docker $USER"

sudo — run this command with administrator privileges. Changing user permissions requires that.
usermod — the Linux command for modifying an existing user account.
-aG — actually two flags combined: -a means "append" (add to, don't remove anything already there), and -G means "supplementary group." Together they mean "add this user to a group, without kicking them out of any group they're already in." That -a matters, if you left it off, this command would replace all of your existing group memberships with just this one, which isn't what you want.
docker — the specific group you're adding yourself to, created when Docker is installed.
$USER — an environment variable that automatically holds your current username (josecyber)  so the command works without typing my username out manually.


![Docker group confirmed and Docker installed](screenshots/26-docker-group-confirmed.png)


