5. This is where Caddy comes into play. Caddy will add a temporary DNS record using my DuckDNS token to prove ownership, which is why I don't need any port open publicly for this to work. This will sit in front of another A record and will handle HTTPS for it, and it will be called a reverse proxy, which is an extremely common piece of the real infrastructure most self-hosted setups and plenty of production companies use for this exact reason.

I need to create the Dockerfile. I need Caddy to know how to talk to DuckDNS out of this box. 

On terminal: "cd ~/vaultwarden
nano Dockerfile"

Then I will type 
"FROM caddy:builder AS builder
RUN xcaddy build --with github.com/caddy-dns/duckdns

FROM caddy:latest
COPY --from=builder /usr/bin/caddy /usr/bin/caddy"

FROM caddy:builder AS builder — starts from Caddy's special "builder" image, which has the Go compiler and build tools needed to compile Caddy from source. We're naming this stage builder so we can reference it later.
RUN xcaddy build --with github.com/caddy-dns/duckdns — xcaddy is Caddy's own official build tool. Stock Caddy doesn't include every possible plugin, so xcaddy recompiles Caddy from source with the specific plugin you ask for bolted in, in this case, the DuckDNS module that lets Caddy prove domain ownership through DuckDNS.
FROM caddy:latest — this starts a second, completely separate image, the normal lightweight Caddy image, no build tools included.
COPY --from=builder /usr/bin/caddy /usr/bin/caddy — this reaches back into the first stage and grabs just the finished Caddy program, no compiler, no source code, no leftover build junk, and drops it into the clean image. This two-stage pattern is called a multi-stage build. The point is the final image stays small and clean, you get the custom binary without shipping the entire toolchain that built it.



6. I now need to create a Caddyfile, and this will be the actual configuration that will tell it what domain to serve, how to get its certificate, and where to send traffic. 

On Terminal: "nano Caddyfile" 
{$DOMAIN} {
    reverse_proxy vaultwarden:80
    tls {
        dns duckdns {env.DUCKDNS_TOKEN}
    }
}

Then Save and Exit. 

7. Now I will create a docker-compose.yml, which is the file that defines and connects both containers. 

On Terminal: nano docker-compose.yml

services:
  vaultwarden:
    image: vaultwarden/server:latest
    container_name: vaultwarden
    restart: unless-stopped
    environment:
      - DOMAIN=https://${DOMAIN}
      - ADMIN_TOKEN=${ADMIN_TOKEN}
    volumes:
      - vw-data:/data
    networks:
      - internal

  caddy:
    build: .
    container_name: caddy
    restart: unless-stopped
    ports:
      - "443:443"
    environment:
      - DOMAIN=${DOMAIN}
      - DUCKDNS_TOKEN=${DUCKDNS_TOKEN}
    volumes:
      - ./Caddyfile:/etc/caddy/Caddyfile
      - caddy-data:/data
      - caddy-config:/config
    depends_on:
      - vaultwarden
    networks:
      - internal

networks:
  internal:

volumes:
  vw-data:
  caddy-data:
  caddy-config:

Save and Exit. 

