I ran into an error.

Error response from daemon: failed to set up container networking: driver failed programming external connectivity on endpoint caddy (Code) failed to bind host port 0.0.0.0:443/tcp: address already in use.

![Port 443 already in use error](screenshots/31-port-443-error.png)

   - Seems like port 443 is in use, but I know thats because of Pi-hole. Let's confirm. Im going to run "sudo ss -tlnp | grep :443"
![Confirming pihole-FTL is holding port 443](screenshots/32-checking-port-443-usage.png)
-t — only show TCP connections (as opposed to UDP or other types).
-l — only show sockets that are listening, meaning actively waiting for incoming connections, not established ones already in progress.
-n — show numeric addresses and port numbers instead of trying to resolve them into names. Makes the output faster and avoids unrelated DNS lookups.
-p — show which process owns each socket, meaning the actual program name and process ID. This is the part that actually answers "what's using this port," without it you'd just see that something is listening on 443, not what.
| grep :443 — the pipe sends the full output of ss into grep, which then filters it down to only lines containing ":443," since ss alone would show you every listening port on the whole system, and you only care about this one.


I will have to tell Pi-hole to stop listening on 443 and keep port 80 for its regular dashboard. Pi-hole listens on both port 80 and 443 for HTTPS access to the dashboard, and it uses a self-signed certificate. I needed to disable port 443 on Pi-hole in order for Caddy to grab port 443 for itself. 

On Terminal: sudo pihole-FTL --config webserver.port "80o,[::]:80o"

That removes the 443os and [::]:443os entries that were in Pi-hole's default config, while keeping port 80 for both IPv4 and IPv6, so its dashboard keeps working over plain HTTP exactly like it always has.


Then restart the service so it can take effect. 

On Terminal: sudo systemctl restart pihole-FTL

And confirm 443 is free.

In Terminal: sudo ss -tlnp | grep :443

![Port 443 confirmed free](screenshots/33-port-443-freed.png)

Now redo step 9.

It started to work, but I've realized I needed an Admin Token.

![ADMIN_TOKEN not set warning](screenshots/34-missing-admin-token-warning.png)

An admin token is basically a password for the special part of Vaultwarden most users will never see in the admin panel. Regular Vaultwarden users log in with their e-mail and master password to reach their own vault. It separates the area meant only for whoever runs the server, and from there you can see every registered user, delete accounts, invite new users, and change server-wide settings.
I need to generate a token because there is no default admin or password baked into Vaultwarden yet. If there were some default password, anyone who's ever set up Vaultwarden before would know that it'd be an open door on every install that uses the default setting. If I generate my own random string with the command "openssl rand -base64 48," this store only has a key that exists only on my machine, and no one else could ever guess or look it up. The reason we are being careful and keeping it out of the screenshots for this particular GitHub is that whoever takes this token effectively holds control over my entire Vaultwarden server, not just one Vault.

![Admin token fixed, both containers started](screenshots/35-admin-token-fixed-containers-started.png)

